<%# "You are offline", said out loud on the one screen where silence costs a session. #516.

    The bug is the whole of htmx's swap rule. A failed request swaps nothing, so a Done tap
    that never reached the server and a Done tap that never happened produce exactly the
    same screen: the row does not tint, the count stays at "2 of 12", and the rest timer
    does not start. Three sets into squats in a basement gym the bars drop to nothing, the
    lifter taps Done, taps it again harder, assumes it did not land, and carries on lifting.
    At the end the app has recorded two sets out of twelve and has said nothing about the
    other ten. The app is not wrong there, it is silent, which on this screen is worse.

    The second casualty is the rest timer, and it is worth naming because it is the thing a
    lifter is actually watching between sets. The countdown is armed by _rest_cue, an element
    the server sends back *with* the Done response and swaps out of band into #rest-cue. No
    response means no cue means no countdown. So losing signal does not only lose the log, it
    takes away the clock -- and a lifter staring at a rest timer that never started has no
    reason at all to suspect the log.

    This is (a) of the three problems #516 separates, and only (a). Nothing here is queued
    and nothing is replayed: that is stage 2 of the issue and it is blocked on an endpoint
    that is safe to send twice, which the complete route is not -- the primary tap toggles,
    deliberately, so a replayed Done marks the set done and then un-does it. Nothing here is
    served from a cache either, which is stage 3 and needs a service worker. What is left
    costs no storage, no service worker and no Ruby, and it is most of the value: it turns a
    silent data-loss bug into an honest one, which is the difference between a lifter who
    knows to re-tap ten sets and a lifter who finds out a week later.

    Background Sync is not the mechanism and could not have been. Safari has never supported
    it on any version or platform, every browser on iOS is WebKit underneath, and the lifter
    this screen exists for is on iOS Safari. The browser specs here drive Firefox, which
    does not support it either -- so the design that works for the user is also the one this
    repo can test, which is a happier alignment than it sounds.

    Fixed to the top of the screen, and that placement is a decision rather than a default.
    The foot of this screen is already three deep -- the RPE footer at z-20, the rest timer
    at z-30, the nudge at z-40 -- and each of those knows the others' heights, in arithmetic
    written into rest_timer_offset and nudge_offset. A fourth would have to be taught to
    every one of them, and worse, it would be the one message that has to be readable at the
    exact moment a rest timer is on screen, because a rest timer that started is not evidence
    of anything when the tap that armed it failed. The top of this screen holds a nav and
    nothing else, and covering the nav while nothing is saving is the right trade: there is
    nowhere worth navigating to until this clears.

    Not a toast, for the same reason. This has to be legible mid-set, on a phone, to somebody
    out of breath, and it has to stay up for as long as it is true rather than for four
    seconds.

    Rendered from session.erb outside #session-body, like the rest timer and the note above
    it, and for the identical reason: a tap replaces one lift panel and the poll replaces
    every panel, and a banner inside that region would be swapped away by the very requests
    it is reporting on -- or, when those requests are failing, left alone by accident, which
    is a correct result arrived at for no reason. %>
<div id="offline-banner" hidden
     class="fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-50 border-b border-amber-200 bg-amber-50">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-md px-4 py-3">
    <%# Two independent lines, shown and hidden separately, because they answer two
        questions that stop being true at different moments. "There is no connection" ends
        the instant a request comes back. "Two sets did not save" ends only when somebody
        taps them again, which may be a minute later and is the thing a lifter has to be
        able to see after the signal returns -- a banner that vanished the moment the wifi
        came back would take the only record of the damage with it. %>
    <p data-offline-state hidden class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-800">
      No connection. Nothing you tap is being saved.
    </p>
    <%# The count rather than a list of which ones, which is what the rows themselves say.
        A number tells a lifter whether to worry, in one glance, from arm's length. %>
    <p data-offline-lost hidden class="mt-1 text-base text-amber-800">
      <span data-offline-count class="font-semibold tabular-nums"></span> did not save.
      Tap Done again on each.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<%# What decides whether any of that is true.

    Every rule here is about preferring evidence over claims, because the one trap in this
    problem is that the browser's own answer is not evidence. navigator.onLine reports
    whether there is a link, not whether anything is at the other end of it: a phone holding
    a useless association with a gym access point, or one behind a captive portal, reads as
    online all day. The asymmetry is what makes it usable at all -- false is trustworthy and
    true is a guess -- so `offline` raises the banner and `online` is deliberately not bound
    to anything. What takes the banner down is a request that came back, which is a fact.

    A request that failed to send is better evidence than the OS either way, and it is what
    the htmx events below provide.

    **The fifteen-second poll is left running, which is where this parts company with #516.**
    That issue asks for the poll to be suspended while offline, through `hx:poll:trigger`,
    on the grounds that it would otherwise machine-gun the banner. There is no such event:
    htmx 2.0.4 polls from a setTimeout that fires the ordinary request events and nothing
    else, so the suspension would have to be a beforeRequest veto rather than the one-line
    hook the issue pictures. Which is worth knowing, because it is not wanted anyway.

    Suspending the poll would take away the only thing on this screen that makes a request
    nobody tapped for -- and that request is what notices the signal has come back. Without
    it the banner could only clear on the next tap, so a lifter resting between sets would
    sit under "no connection" for two minutes after the bars returned, which is the same
    lie in the other direction. Kept running, it is a heartbeat: it finds the outage within
    fifteen seconds, usually before a single set has been lost to it, and it finds the way
    back just as fast. The machine-gunning it would cause is real and is handled where it
    belongs, in what is said rather than in what is requested -- a failure nobody tapped for
    raises the banner silently and announces nothing. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var banner = document.getElementById('offline-banner');
    if (!banner) { return; }

    var state = banner.querySelector('[data-offline-state]');
    var lost = banner.querySelector('[data-offline-lost]');
    var counter = banner.querySelector('[data-offline-count]');
    // The rows whose tap never reached the server, by set id. A plain object rather than a
    // Set because the only thing ever asked of it is membership, and because the rest of
    // the scripts on this screen are written in the same dialect.
    var unsaved = {};
    var offline = navigator.onLine === false;

    // Which set a request was about, read off the path it was sent to rather than off the
    // DOM around the element that sent it.
    //
    // #516 makes this point about the write queue it declines to build -- requestConfig
    // carries the verb, the path and the parameters, so nothing is ever reconstructed from
    // markup -- and it turns out to be load-bearing here for a reason the issue does not
    // reach. When a request succeeds, htmx fires afterRequest on the element that sent it,
    // and by then the swap has already taken that element out of the document. An event
    // fired on a detached node does not bubble to a listener on the body, so this script
    // never sees that one at all; htmx notices and re-fires on the nearest ancestor still
    // on the page, which on this screen is #lift-panels. That is the right request arriving
    // with every trace of which row it came from removed. The path still has it.
    //
    // Null for everything that is not one set being completed: the poller, and the two
    // forms that add and swap a lift, which make rows rather than finish them.
    function setOf(event) {
      var config = event.detail && event.detail.requestConfig;
      var found = config && config.path && config.path.match(/\/sets\/(\d+)\/complete/);
      return found && found[1];
    }

    function mark(id, shown) {
      var row = document.querySelector('[data-set-row="' + id + '"]');
      var note = row && row.querySelector('[data-unsaved]');
      if (note) { note.hidden = !shown; }
    }

    // Draws the whole of what this script knows, every time, rather than patching the bit
    // that changed. There are two lines and a set of row marks and four events that can
    // move any of them; re-deciding all of it from the two variables above is shorter than
    // the transitions would be, and it cannot leave the banner disagreeing with the rows.
    function render() {
      var ids = Object.keys(unsaved);
      counter.textContent = ids.length === 1 ? '1 set' : ids.length + ' sets';
      // Re-applied here rather than left to survive, because they cannot: the poll
      // re-renders every panel whenever anything on the screen has changed, and a mark
      // comes back from the server hidden. The server is right to send it hidden -- the row
      // is not saved as far as this browser is concerned, and the database has never heard
      // of the tap -- so remembering is this script's job.
      for (var i = 0; i < ids.length; i++) { mark(ids[i], true); }
      state.hidden = !offline;
      lost.hidden = ids.length === 0;
      banner.hidden = state.hidden && lost.hidden;
    }

    // Through the region the server already announces every tap into (#336) rather than a
    // second live region of this element's own. A lifter who cannot see the banner hears
    // this in the same place, and in the same breath, as "squat, set 3 of 5, done".
    function announce(sentence) {
      var region = document.getElementById('session-announcement');
      if (region) { region.textContent = sentence; }
    }

    // A request that never reached the server. htmx fires sendError for a connection that
    // could not be made and timeout for one that was made and never answered -- one or the
    // other, never both -- and for this screen's purposes they are the same fact: the tap
    // is not in the database and the lifter has no way of knowing that.
    function failed(event) {
      var id = setOf(event);
      var fresh = !offline;
      offline = true;
      if (id) { unsaved[id] = true; }
      render();
      // The poll is the one request on this screen nobody tapped for, so its failure raises
      // the banner and says nothing further: there is no row to mark, and repeating the
      // same sentence every fifteen seconds is what would machine-gun a screen reader. The
      // first one is worth saying, which is what `fresh` is for.
      if (id) {
        announce('Offline. ' + counter.textContent + ' did not save.');
      } else if (fresh) {
        announce('Offline. Nothing you tap is being saved.');
      }
    }

    // A response that came back, whatever it said. A 404 or a 500 is the server disagreeing
    // with the request, and disagreeing is something only a reachable server can do, so an
    // error response is proof of a connection in exactly the way a failure to send is proof
    // of none. This is also why htmx:responseError is bound to nothing: showing "you are
    // offline" because the server refused a write would be a second lie replacing the first.
    function returned(event) {
      var xhr = event.detail && event.detail.xhr;
      // status 0 is a request that never got an answer, which arrives here too -- htmx
      // fires afterRequest before sendError on the same failure.
      if (!xhr || !xhr.status) { return; }
      var id = setOf(event);
      var was = offline;
      offline = false;
      // Only the row this request was about. Every other mark stands, because the
      // connection coming back is not somebody having lifted those sets again -- they are
      // still not in the database, and this is the only thing on the screen saying so.
      if (id && unsaved[id]) {
        delete unsaved[id];
        mark(id, false);
      }
      render();
      // Said once, on the way back, and only when it was the poll that found out. Where the
      // lifter's own tap is what came back, the server has sent its own sentence into this
      // region already -- overwriting "squat, set 3 of 5, done" with "back online" would
      // replace the confirmation they were waiting for with news they can see in the banner.
      if (was && !id) {
        announce(Object.keys(unsaved).length ?
          'Back online. ' + counter.textContent + ' still not saved.' : 'Back online.');
      }
    }

    // Bound to document.body because htmx events bubble, so this binds once and survives
    // every swap -- the same reasoning as the two scripts at the foot of session.erb, and
    // load-bearing here in a way it is not there: the elements these events come from are
    // the forms inside the panels, which are replaced on every tap and every poll.
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:sendError', failed);
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:timeout', failed);
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterRequest', returned);
    // And once more after every swap, because a swap is the one way a mark can be lost
    // without anything having happened to the set it is about.
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', render);

    // The browser volunteering that the link has gone, which is the one thing it says about
    // the network that can be believed. Said once: a phone that flaps between cells fires
    // this repeatedly, and the sentence is the same every time.
    window.addEventListener('offline', function () {
      if (offline) { return; }
      offline = true;
      render();
      announce('Offline. Nothing you tap is being saved.');
    });

    render();
  })();
</script>
